{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MmDZZAKsgd7KtQyxaDnh8B","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Abandon dual dynamics as headline contribution. Reframe paper\n2\t  around two angles that survive: (1) Task formulation paper — ''Generation Depth\n3\t  Estimation: A Missing Layer in Synthetic Text Analysis.'' The task itself (ordinal\n4\t  depth, not binary) is novel regardless of feature performance. Formalize the task,\n5\t  provide baselines, release benchmark dataset, discuss what makes it hard. (2) Negative/analytical\n6\t  result on depth signal scaling — depth signal is strong for small generators but\n7\t  degrades with scale, revealing that distributional drift from recursive generation\n8\t  is partially masked by model fluency. Connects to model collapse literature. Perplexity-by-depth\n9\t  monotonic decrease (13.5→1.7) remains robust as reference-model phenomenon — reposition\n10\t  around ''reference-model perplexity as depth proxy'' rather than the full dual dynamics\n11\t  story. Paper becomes: new task + benchmark + scaling analysis + practical baseline.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: feature_ablation_and_trend\n16\t  plan_id: plan_001\n17\t  description: 'Feature ablation (surface-only vs reference-only vs full) and H1/H2\n18\t    trend analysis on MVP data. Already executed; registry results from plan-001-fix\n19\t    are taken as given. In worst tier, these in-distribution results are not wrong\n20\t    but are misleadingly optimistic — the 0.06 full-vs-surface gap and the clean H1/H2\n21\t    split both depend on same-family (Pythia) reference and single-generator setting.\n22\t\n23\t    '\n24\t  expected_results:\n25\t    full_macro_f1: 0.8204\n26\t    surface_only_f1: 0.7628\n27\t    reference_only_f1: 0.7187\n28\t    full_vs_surface_gap: 0.058\n29\t    full_vs_reference_gap: 0.102\n30\t    h2_features_count: 3\n31\t    h1_features_count: 4\n32\t    entropy_mean_depth_rho: -0.68\n33\t    median_ppl_by_depth: 13.50, 4.96, 3.00, 2.07, 1.72\n34\t  result_rationale: 'These results are from actual execution and look encouraging.\n35\t    But they are entirely in-distribution: single generator (Pythia-2.8B), same-family\n36\t    reference (Pythia-410M), no length control. The 0.82 full F1 benefits from generator-specific\n37\t    artifacts that the reference model can detect precisely because it shares the\n38\t    Pythia architecture. The H2 entropy_mean drift (ρ=-0.68) is strong here because\n39\t    Pythia-410M and Pythia-2.8B share tokenizer and distributional priors — the reference\n40\t    model''s perplexity is calibrated to the generator''s output distribution. The\n41\t    perplexity monotonic decrease (13.5→1.7) is real but partially reflects the reference\n42\t    model''s familiarity with same-family outputs. In worst tier, subsequent experiments\n43\t    reveal these as inflated baselines.\n44\t\n45\t    '\n46\t  tables:\n47\t  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation (MVP In-Distribution)'\n48\t    columns:\n49\t    - feature_set\n50\t    - macro_f1\n51\t    - 95_ci\n52\t    - gen0_f1\n53\t    - gen1_f1\n54\t    - gen2plus_f1\n55\t    rows:\n56\t    - - full\n57\t      - 0.8204\n58\t      - '[0.807, 0.834]'\n59\t      - 0.952\n60\t      - 0.776\n61\t      - 0.795\n62\t    - - surface_only\n63\t      - 0.7628\n64\t      - '[0.747, 0.774]'\n65\t      - 0.92\n66\t      - 0.71\n67\t      - 0.658\n68\t    - - reference_only\n69\t      - 0.7187\n70\t      - '[0.706, 0.733]'\n71\t      - 0.895\n72\t      - 0.64\n73\t      - 0.621\n74\t  - name: 'Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Classification'\n75\t    columns:\n76\t    - feature\n77\t    - source\n78\t    - depth_slope\n79\t    - R2\n80\t    - classification\n81\t    rows:\n82\t    - - entropy_mean\n83\t      - reference\n84\t      - -0.042\n85\t      - 0.91\n86\t      - H2_drift\n87\t    - - ttr\n88\t      - surface\n89\t      - -0.031\n90\t      - 0.87\n91\t      - H2_drift\n92\t    - - hapax_ratio\n93\t      - surface\n94\t      - -0.028\n95\t      - 0.83\n96\t      - H2_drift\n97\t    - - entropy_std\n98\t      - reference\n99\t      - 0.003\n100\t      - 0.04\n101\t      - H1_stationary\n102\t    - - entropy_max\n103\t      - reference\n104\t      - -0.005\n105\t      - 0.07\n106\t      - H1_stationary\n107\t    - - entropy_min\n108\t      - reference\n109\t      - 0.002\n110\t      - 0.02\n111\t      - H1_stationary\n112\t    - - tail_decay\n113\t      - reference\n114\t      - 0.004\n115\t      - 0.05\n116\t      - H1_stationary\n117\t  figures_description:\n118\t  - 'Fig 1: Feature trends across depth 0-4 with mean±std bands. Clear monotonic decrease\n119\t    for entropy_mean/TTR/hapax_ratio; flat for shape features. Looks clean — but this\n120\t    is single-generator same-family setting.'\n121\t  - 'Fig 2: Perplexity by depth (Pythia-410M reference). Monotonic 13.5→1.7. Strong\n122\t    signal — survives into worst tier as reference-model-specific phenomenon.'\n123\t  analysis: 'In-distribution results are solid. The dual dynamics split appears clean:\n124\t    3 H2 features drift, 4 H1 features are stationary. Perplexity signal is very strong.\n125\t    However, the worst-tier interpretation is that this clean separation is partly\n126\t    an artifact of the Pythia-Pythia configuration. Subsequent experiments (plan_003,\n127\t    plan_013) will show the H2 drift is partially confounded by length artifacts and\n128\t    reference-model specificity.\n129\t\n130\t    '\n131\t- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n132\t  plan_id: plan_002\n133\t  description: 'Leave-one-generator-out evaluation across 5 generators. Tests cross-generator\n134\t    generalization. In worst tier, LOGO F1 is poor, especially when holding out non-Pythia\n135\t    generators.\n136\t\n137\t    '\n138\t  expected_results:\n139\t    overall_logo_macro_f1: 0.52\n140\t    logo_holdout_pythia28b: 0.61\n141\t    logo_holdout_pythia1b: 0.64\n142\t    logo_holdout_olmo2: 0.48\n143\t    logo_holdout_qwen17b: 0.44\n144\t    logo_holdout_qwen06b: 0.43\n145\t    within_family_avg_f1: 0.625\n146\t    cross_family_avg_f1: 0.45\n147\t    leave_one_family_out_pythia_held_in: 0.58\n148\t    leave_one_family_out_pythia_held_out: 0.41\n149\t  result_rationale: 'The classifier overfits to generator-specific artifacts rather\n150\t    than learning universal depth signatures. Within the Pythia family, generalization\n151\t    is passable (0.61–0.64) because Pythia-2.8B and Pythia-1B share tokenizer, training\n152\t    data (The Pile), and architectural patterns — the Pythia-410M reference model''s\n153\t    NLL features are structurally similar for both. But cross-family holdouts (OLMo,\n154\t    Qwen) drop sharply to 0.43–0.48 because: (a) different tokenizers mean surface\n155\t    features (TTR, hapax) have different baseline distributions, (b) the Pythia-410M\n156\t    reference model assigns systematically different perplexity distributions to non-Pythia\n157\t    outputs, creating a domain shift in entropy features, (c) each generator family\n158\t    has its own repetition/diversity profile at each depth, so depth-correlated patterns\n159\t    learned from one family don''t transfer. The leave-one-family-out result (0.41\n160\t    when Pythia held out) confirms the classifier critically depends on Pythia-specific\n161\t    patterns. Overall LOGO F1 of 0.52 is below the 0.58 threshold, killing the cross-generator\n162\t    generalization claim.\n163\t\n164\t    '\n165\t  tables:\n166\t  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Macro-F1'\n167\t    columns:\n168\t    - held_out_generator\n169\t    - family\n170\t    - macro_f1\n171\t    - gen0_f1\n172\t    - gen1_f1\n173\t    - gen2plus_f1\n174\t    rows:\n175\t    - - pythia-2.8b\n176\t      - pythia\n177\t      - 0.61\n178\t      - 0.82\n179\t      - 0.51\n180\t      - 0.5\n181\t    - - pythia-1b\n182\t      - pythia\n183\t      - 0.64\n184\t      - 0.85\n185\t      - 0.54\n186\t      - 0.53\n187\t    - - olmo-2-1b\n188\t      - olmo\n189\t      - 0.48\n190\t      - 0.72\n191\t      - 0.38\n192\t      - 0.34\n193\t    - - qwen3-1.7b\n194\t      - qwen\n195\t      - 0.44\n196\t      - 0.68\n197\t      - 0.35\n198\t      - 0.29\n199\t    - - qwen3-0.6b\n200\t      - qwen\n201\t      - 0.43\n202\t      - 0.67\n203\t      - 0.33\n204\t      - 0.29\n205\t    - - mean (all)\n206\t      - '-'\n207\t      - 0.52\n208\t      - 0.75\n209\t      - 0.42\n210\t      - 0.39\n211\t  - name: 'Table 4: Leave-One-Family-Out'\n212\t    columns:\n213\t    - held_out_family\n214\t    - train_generators\n215\t    - macro_f1\n216\t    rows:\n217\t    - - pythia\n218\t      - olmo+qwen\n219\t      - 0.41\n220\t    - - olmo\n221\t      - pythia+qwen\n222\t      - 0.54\n223\t    - - qwen\n224\t      - pythia+olmo\n225\t      - 0.56\n226\t  figures_description:\n227\t  - 'Fig 3: Confusion matrices for each LOGO fold. Pythia holdouts show reasonable\n228\t    diagonal; OLMo/Qwen holdouts show heavy off-diagonal mass, especially gen1↔gen2+\n229\t    confusion.'\n230\t  - 'Fig 4: Feature distribution shift across generators. Box plots of entropy_mean\n231\t    by depth for each generator — Pythia generators show clean separation, OLMo/Qwen\n232\t    show overlapping distributions.'\n233\t  analysis: 'Cross-generator generalization fails the 0.58 threshold. The within-family\n234\t    vs cross-family gap (0.625 vs 0.45) reveals the classifier relies on generator-specific\n235\t    artifacts. Gen-0 detection remains passable (~0.75 average) because human-written\n236\t    text is universally distinct, but gen-1 vs gen-2+ discrimination (the core depth\n237\t    signal) collapses cross-family. This undermines Claim 1''s generalization requirement.\n238\t    In the reframed paper, this becomes evidence for: \"depth estimation requires generator-aware\n239\t    features or larger, more diverse training sets — the task is harder than binary\n240\t    detection because depth artifacts are generator-specific.\"\n241\t\n242\t    '\n243\t- name: cross_family_reference_model\n244\t  plan_id: plan_003\n245\t  description: 'Test non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) for feature\n246\t    extraction. Critical falsification test for the dual dynamics framework.\n247\t\n248\t    '\n249\t  expected_results:\n250\t    olmo_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.22\n251\t    qwen_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.15\n252\t    pythia_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.68\n253\t    olmo_ref_full_f1: 0.63\n254\t    qwen_ref_full_f1: 0.58\n255\t    pythia_ref_full_f1: 0.82\n256\t    olmo_ref_h2_features_count: 1\n257\t    qwen_ref_h2_features_count: 1\n258\t    cross_family_ref_entropy_mean_rho_avg: -0.185\n259\t    shape_features_h1_stable_across_refs: true\n260\t    ttr_rho_across_refs: -0.3\n261\t    hapax_rho_across_refs: -0.27\n262\t  result_rationale: 'Entropy_mean drift (ρ=-0.68 with Pythia reference) largely disappears\n263\t    with non-Pythia references (ρ≈-0.22 OLMo, ρ≈-0.15 Qwen). This happens because\n264\t    entropy_mean is driven by how well the reference model predicts the generated\n265\t    text — when the reference model shares architecture and training data with the\n266\t    generator (Pythia-Pythia), it has calibrated predictions that systematically shift\n267\t    with depth. A cross-family reference model assigns noisier, less calibrated perplexities,\n268\t    washing out the drift signal. The |ρ|<0.3 threshold is breached for both non-Pythia\n269\t    references. Shape features (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain H1-stationary\n270\t    across all references — this is genuinely robust because distributional shape\n271\t    is less sensitive to absolute calibration. But the H2 side of the \"dual dynamics\"\n272\t    now has only surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) showing reliable drift (ρ≈-0.28\n273\t    to -0.30), and these are precisely the features confounded by text length (plan_013).\n274\t    The \"dual dynamics cross-cuts surface/reference boundary\" claim collapses: the\n275\t    cross-cutting was an artifact of Pythia-Pythia alignment. Classification F1 with\n276\t    non-Pythia reference drops to 0.58–0.63 because the strongest H2 reference feature\n277\t    is now noise.\n278\t\n279\t    '\n280\t  tables:\n281\t  - name: 'Table 5: Entropy_mean Depth Correlation by Reference Model'\n282\t    columns:\n283\t    - reference_model\n284\t    - family\n285\t    - entropy_mean_rho\n286\t    - p_value\n287\t    - h2_classification\n288\t    rows:\n289\t    - - pythia-410m\n290\t      - pythia\n291\t      - -0.68\n292\t      - <0.001\n293\t      - H2_confirmed\n294\t    - - olmo-2-1b\n295\t      - olmo\n296\t      - -0.22\n297\t      - 0.031\n298\t      - H2_weak (|ρ|<0.3)\n299\t    - - qwen3-0.6b\n300\t      - qwen\n301\t      - -0.15\n302\t      - 0.118\n303\t      - H2_not_confirmed\n304\t  - name: 'Table 6: Full Feature Set Classification by Reference Model'\n305\t    columns:\n306\t    - reference_model\n307\t    - macro_f1\n308\t    - surface_only_f1\n309\t    - ref_only_f1\n310\t    - full_vs_surface_gap\n311\t    rows:\n312\t    - - pythia-410m\n313\t      - 0.82\n314\t      - 0.76\n315\t      - 0.72\n316\t      - 0.06\n317\t    - - olmo-2-1b\n318\t      - 0.63\n319\t      - 0.61\n320\t      - 0.48\n321\t      - 0.02\n322\t    - - qwen3-0.6b\n323\t      - 0.58\n324\t      - 0.57\n325\t      - 0.41\n326\t      - 0.01\n327\t  - name: 'Table 7: H1/H2 Stability Across Reference Models'\n328\t    columns:\n329\t    - feature\n330\t    - pythia_class\n331\t    - olmo_class\n332\t    - qwen_class\n333\t    - stable_across_refs\n334\t    rows:\n335\t    - - entropy_mean\n336\t      - H2\n337\t      - weak/NS\n338\t      - NS\n339\t      - 'NO'\n340\t    - - entropy_std\n341\t      - H1\n342\t      - H1\n343\t      - H1\n344\t      - 'YES'\n345\t    - - entropy_max\n346\t      - H1\n347\t      - H1\n348\t      - H1\n349\t      - 'YES'\n350\t    - - tail_decay\n351\t      - H1\n352\t      - H1\n353\t      - H1\n354\t      - 'YES'\n355\t    - - ttr\n356\t      - H2\n357\t      - H2\n358\t      - H2\n359\t      - 'YES'\n360\t    - - hapax_ratio\n361\t      - H2\n362\t      - H2\n363\t      - weak H2\n364\t      - YES (marginal)\n365\t  figures_description:\n366\t  - 'Fig 5: Entropy_mean by depth for 3 reference models. Pythia shows clear monotonic\n367\t    decrease; OLMo shows slight noisy decline; Qwen nearly flat. Devastating for the\n368\t    dual dynamics narrative.'\n369\t  - 'Fig 6: Shape features (entropy_std, tail_decay) by depth for 3 reference models.\n370\t    All show flat trends — H1 stationarity is genuinely reference-model-independent.'\n371\t  analysis: 'The key falsification test triggers: entropy_mean''s H2 drift was substantially\n372\t    a Pythia-specific artifact. The dual dynamics narrative (\"level features drift,\n373\t    shape features don''t, and this cross-cuts surface/reference\") collapses because\n374\t    the cross-cutting evidence depended on entropy_mean being a reference feature\n375\t    that drifts. Without it, H2 features are just surface features (TTR, hapax), and\n376\t    H1 features are just reference features — exactly the naive expectation the paper\n377\t    claimed to overturn. The shape feature H1 stability is real but less interesting:\n378\t    it says \"distributional shape under any reference model is stable across depth,\"\n379\t    which is a weaker claim. In the reframed paper, this becomes: \"reference-model\n380\t    perplexity as depth proxy is reference-model-dependent — a same-family reference\n381\t    provides much stronger signal, pointing toward an alignment-based explanation\n382\t    of depth discriminability.\"\n383\t\n384\t    '\n385\t- name: downstream_utility\n386\t  plan_id: plan_004\n387\t  description: 'Fine-tune OLMo-1B on depth-stratified data. Compare depth-aware filtering\n388\t    vs binary baselines with 3+ seeds and bootstrap CIs. In worst tier, the advantage\n389\t    is not statistically significant.\n390\t\n391\t    '\n392\t  expected_results:\n393\t    depth_quality_spearman: 0.28\n394\t    depth_quality_spearman_ci: '[0.11, 0.45]'\n395\t    depth_filtering_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.8\n396\t    depth_filtering_ppl_improvement_ci: '[-0.5, 4.1]'\n397\t    binary_filtering_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.2\n398\t    binary_filtering_ppl_improvement_ci: '[-1.1, 3.5]'\n399\t    depth_vs_binary_p_value: 0.31\n400\t    sample_matched_depth_ppl: 14.2\n401\t    sample_matched_binary_ppl: 14.5\n402\t    sample_matched_p_value: 0.42\n403\t    seed_variance_cv: 0.08\n404\t    n_seeds: 5\n405\t  result_rationale: 'With proper experimental controls (multiple seeds, CIs, sample-count\n406\t    matching, non-Pythia model), the downstream advantage of depth-aware filtering\n407\t    shrinks to statistical insignificance. Several factors contribute: (1) OLMo-1B\n408\t    is more robust to training data contamination than Pythia-410M (the original MVP\n409\t    model), reducing the overall signal magnitude; (2) with 5 seeds, the variance\n410\t    across runs is substantial (CV≈0.08), swamping the small depth-vs-binary difference;\n411\t    (3) the sample-count-matched comparison shows the original advantage was partly\n412\t    a data size confound — depth-aware filtering keeps more data (it retains gen-1)\n413\t    than binary filtering (which discards all synthetic), so the improvement was partially\n414\t    from having more training data, not from depth stratification per se; (4) bootstrap\n415\t    CIs for the depth-vs-binary comparison overlap substantially (p=0.31), failing\n416\t    significance. The Spearman ρ=0.28 between predicted depth and quality degradation\n417\t    is real but weak, falling in the [0.15, 0.4] worst-tier range. In isolation, this\n418\t    correlation is interesting but not actionable. Claim 3 (downstream utility) cannot\n419\t    be supported as \"significantly outperforms binary baselines.\"\n420\t\n421\t    '\n422\t  tables:\n423\t  - name: 'Table 8: Downstream Fine-Tuning Results (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n424\t    columns:\n425\t    - filtering_strategy\n426\t    - mean_ppl\n427\t    - std_ppl\n428\t    - 95_ci\n429\t    - improvement_vs_unfiltered_pct\n430\t    rows:\n431\t    - - unfiltered\n432\t      - 14.8\n433\t      - 0.42\n434\t      - '[14.2, 15.4]'\n435\t      - '-'\n436\t    - - binary_fastdetectgpt\n437\t      - 14.5\n438\t      - 0.51\n439\t      - '[13.8, 15.2]'\n440\t      - 1.2 [-1.1, 3.5]\n441\t    - - binary_ldscore\n442\t      - 14.6\n443\t      - 0.48\n444\t      - '[14.0, 15.2]'\n445\t      - 0.9 [-1.4, 3.2]\n446\t    - - depth_aware_keep_gen01\n447\t      - 14.2\n448\t      - 0.39\n449\t      - '[13.7, 14.7]'\n450\t      - 1.8 [-0.5, 4.1]\n451\t    - - depth_aware_sample_matched\n452\t      - 14.2\n453\t      - 0.44\n454\t      - '[13.6, 14.8]'\n455\t      - 1.5 [-0.8, 3.8]\n456\t  - name: 'Table 9: Depth-Quality Correlation'\n457\t    columns:\n458\t    - metric\n459\t    - value\n460\t    - 95_ci\n461\t    rows:\n462\t    - - spearman_rho\n463\t      - 0.28\n464\t      - '[0.11, 0.45]'\n465\t    - - ppl_gen0_mean\n466\t      - 12.1\n467\t      - '[11.8, 12.4]'\n468\t    - - ppl_gen1_mean\n469\t      - 13.4\n470\t      - '[12.9, 13.9]'\n471\t    - - ppl_gen2_mean\n472\t      - 14.1\n473\t      - '[13.5, 14.7]'\n474\t    - - ppl_gen3_mean\n475\t      - 14.5\n476\t      - '[13.8, 15.2]'\n477\t    - - ppl_gen4_mean\n478\t      - 14.9\n479\t      - '[14.1, 15.7]'\n480\t  figures_description:\n481\t  - 'Fig 7: Box plots of held-out perplexity across 5 seeds for each filtering strategy.\n482\t    Wide overlapping boxes; depth-aware slightly lower median but CIs encompass binary.'\n483\t  - 'Fig 8: Training data depth composition vs downstream perplexity. Weak positive\n484\t    trend (ρ=0.28) with substantial scatter.'\n485\t  analysis: 'Downstream utility claim fails significance after proper experimental\n486\t    controls. The improvement exists directionally (1.8% vs 1.2%) but CIs overlap.\n487\t    This was the most anticipated outcome from dream reviews: single-run results are\n488\t    fragile. In reframed paper, report honestly as \"directional evidence for depth-quality\n489\t    correlation, insufficient for statistical significance at this sample size\" and\n490\t    pivot to the correlation itself (ρ=0.28) as motivation for future work. The perplexity-by-depth\n491\t    monotonic relationship (Table 9) is real and publishable as a descriptive finding\n492\t    — it just doesn''t translate into significant downstream filtering advantage.\n493\t\n494\t    '\n495\t- name: prompt_type_comparison\n496\t  plan_id: plan_005\n497\t  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n498\t    In worst tier, depth signal varies dramatically by prompt type, undermining generalizability.\n499\t\n500\t    '\n501\t  expected_results:\n502\t    continuation_macro_f1: 0.78\n503\t    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.55\n504\t    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.47\n505\t    continuation_entropy_mean_rho: -0.65\n506\t    elaboration_entropy_mean_rho: -0.38\n507\t    rewriting_entropy_mean_rho: -0.18\n508\t    cross_prompt_train_test_f1: 0.42\n509\t  result_rationale: 'Depth signal is strongly prompt-type dependent. Continuation\n510\t    chains preserve the strongest signal (F1=0.78) because each generation builds\n511\t    directly on the previous output, creating cumulative distributional drift. Elaboration\n512\t    chains weaken the signal (F1=0.55) because the model is asked to expand on a topic\n513\t    rather than continue text — this introduces more creative variation that partially\n514\t    resets the distributional state at each depth. Inspired rewriting shows near-chance\n515\t    depth discrimination (F1=0.47) because the semantic departure from the source\n516\t    is maximal — each depth step is essentially an independent generation conditioned\n517\t    on a theme, not a recursive chain. The entropy_mean drift follows the same pattern\n518\t    (ρ: -0.65/-0.38/-0.18). When training on one prompt type and testing on another,\n519\t    F1 drops to 0.42, showing the features are prompt-type-specific. This means the\n520\t    \"depth signal\" is partly a \"continuation-chain-specific\" artifact — real web contamination\n521\t    involves diverse generation patterns, not just continuation.\n522\t\n523\t    '\n524\t  tables:\n525\t  - name: 'Table 10: Depth Classification by Prompt Type'\n526\t    columns:\n527\t    - prompt_type\n528\t    - macro_f1\n529\t    - entropy_mean_rho\n530\t    - ttr_rho\n531\t    - gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1\n532\t    rows:\n533\t    - - continuation\n534\t      - 0.78\n535\t      - -0.65\n536\t      - -0.29\n537\t      - 0.71\n538\t    - - elaboration\n539\t      - 0.55\n540\t      - -0.38\n541\t      - -0.16\n542\t      - 0.44\n543\t    - - inspired_rewriting\n544\t      - 0.47\n545\t      - -0.18\n546\t      - -0.08\n547\t      - 0.36\n548\t  - name: 'Table 11: Cross-Prompt Transfer'\n549\t    columns:\n550\t    - train_prompt\n551\t    - test_prompt\n552\t    - macro_f1\n553\t    rows:\n554\t    - - continuation\n555\t      - elaboration\n556\t      - 0.46\n557\t    - - continuation\n558\t      - rewriting\n559\t      - 0.39\n560\t    - - elaboration\n561\t      - continuation\n562\t      - 0.51\n563\t    - - elaboration\n564\t      - rewriting\n565\t      - 0.42\n566\t    - - rewriting\n567\t      - continuation\n568\t      - 0.44\n569\t    - - rewriting\n570\t      - elaboration\n571\t      - 0.4\n572\t  figures_description:\n573\t  - 'Fig 9: Feature drift (entropy_mean, TTR) by depth for 3 prompt types. Continuation\n574\t    shows monotonic decrease; elaboration shows noisy partial decrease; rewriting\n575\t    shows flat/noisy trajectory.'\n576\t  analysis: 'Prompt type is a major moderator of depth signal, challenging ecological\n577\t    validity. In the reframed paper, this becomes a positive finding for the task\n578\t    formulation angle: \"Generation depth estimation difficulty depends critically\n579\t    on the semantic coupling between generation steps. Continuation chains (high coupling)\n580\t    preserve depth signal; rewriting chains (low coupling) do not. This connects to\n581\t    attractor cycle theory — semantic-preserving chains converge, while semantically\n582\t    divergent chains don''t accumulate distributional drift.\"\n583\t\n584\t    '\n585\t- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n586\t  plan_id: plan_006\n587\t  description: 'Compare paraphrasing vs continuation chains. In worst tier, paraphrasing\n588\t    chains converge quickly (as attractor theory predicts), but continuation chains\n589\t    also show limited depth discrimination beyond gen-2.\n590\t\n591\t    '\n592\t  expected_results:\n593\t    paraphrastic_3class_f1: 0.52\n594\t    nonparaphrastic_3class_f1: 0.78\n595\t    paraphrastic_entropy_mean_plateau_depth: 2\n596\t    nonparaphrastic_saturation_depth: 3\n597\t    paraphrastic_gen2_vs_gen3_f1: 0.34\n598\t    nonparaphrastic_gen2_vs_gen3_f1: 0.41\n599\t  result_rationale: 'Paraphrasing chains converge by depth 2 as attractor theory predicts\n600\t    — gen-2, gen-3, gen-4 are nearly indistinguishable (F1=0.34 for gen-2 vs gen-3).\n601\t    Continuation chains preserve more signal but also saturate by depth 3 — the distributional\n602\t    drift accumulates but with diminishing marginal signal at each step. The worst-tier\n603\t    interpretation: even non-paraphrastic chains have bounded depth discriminability,\n604\t    just with a slightly higher ceiling than paraphrastic ones. The difference (0.78\n605\t    vs 0.52) confirms continuation chains are better for depth estimation but doesn''t\n606\t    rescue the broader generalizability problem — the signal is still chain-type-specific\n607\t    and bounded.\n608\t\n609\t    '\n610\t  tables:\n611\t  - name: 'Table 12: Paraphrastic vs Non-Paraphrastic Depth Discrimination'\n612\t    columns:\n613\t    - chain_type\n614\t    - 3class_f1\n615\t    - gen0_f1\n616\t    - gen1_f1\n617\t    - gen2plus_f1\n618\t    - entropy_mean_rho\n619\t    rows:\n620\t    - - paraphrastic\n621\t      - 0.52\n622\t      - 0.78\n623\t      - 0.45\n624\t      - 0.33\n625\t      - -0.31\n626\t    - - continuation\n627\t      - 0.78\n628\t      - 0.93\n629\t      - 0.72\n630\t      - 0.69\n631\t      - -0.65\n632\t  figures_description:\n633\t  - 'Fig 10: Feature values by depth for paraphrastic vs continuation chains. Paraphrastic\n634\t    shows rapid convergence (plateau by depth 2); continuation shows gradual drift\n635\t    with diminishing returns after depth 3.'\n636\t  analysis: 'Confirms attractor theory prediction for paraphrastic chains. Continuation\n637\t    chains have more signal but also saturate. In reframed paper: \"Our empirical results\n638\t    are consistent with attractor convergence — extending the theory to non-paraphrastic\n639\t    chains shows the convergence is slower but still present, bounding practical depth\n640\t    estimation to ~3 useful classes.\"\n641\t\n642\t    '\n643\t- name: cross_model_chains\n644\t  plan_id: plan_007\n645\t  description: 'Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step). In worst\n646\t    tier, cross-model chains destroy depth signal because generator identity dominates\n647\t    depth identity.\n648\t\n649\t    '\n650\t  expected_results:\n651\t    cross_model_macro_f1: 0.39\n652\t    single_model_macro_f1: 0.78\n653\t    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.65\n654\t    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.28\n655\t    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.24\n656\t    cross_vs_single_delta: -0.39\n657\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are dramatically harder (F1=0.39, essentially\n658\t    near chance for gen-1 and gen-2+). The reason is fundamental: when generator identity\n659\t    changes at each depth step, the distributional features capture generator identity\n660\t    more than depth. A gen-1 text from Qwen looks more different from gen-1 text from\n661\t    Pythia than gen-1 looks from gen-2 within the same generator. The features (NLL\n662\t    statistics under a fixed reference model) are sensitive to the generator''s output\n663\t    distribution, creating a confound where generator variation swamps depth variation.\n664\t    Gen-0 remains detectable (F1=0.65) because human-written text is still distinct\n665\t    from any machine-generated text, but ordinal depth discrimination collapses. This\n666\t    is perhaps the strongest negative result: in the realistic scenario of diverse\n667\t    web generators, depth estimation with these features is not viable.\n668\t\n669\t    '\n670\t  tables:\n671\t  - name: 'Table 13: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chains'\n672\t    columns:\n673\t    - chain_type\n674\t    - macro_f1\n675\t    - gen0_f1\n676\t    - gen1_f1\n677\t    - gen2plus_f1\n678\t    rows:\n679\t    - - single_model\n680\t      - 0.78\n681\t      - 0.93\n682\t      - 0.72\n683\t      - 0.69\n684\t    - - cross_model_random\n685\t      - 0.39\n686\t      - 0.65\n687\t      - 0.28\n688\t      - 0.24\n689\t    - - cross_model_increasing_size\n690\t      - 0.41\n691\t      - 0.66\n692\t      - 0.3\n693\t      - 0.27\n694\t    - - cross_model_decreasing_size\n695\t      - 0.38\n696\t      - 0.63\n697\t      - 0.27\n698\t      - 0.24\n699\t  figures_description:\n700\t  - 'Fig 11: t-SNE of feature space colored by depth, single-model (left) vs cross-model\n701\t    (right). Single-model shows depth clusters; cross-model shows generator clusters.'\n702\t  analysis: 'Cross-model chains destroy depth signal — generator identity is a much\n703\t    stronger signal than depth in the feature space. This is the ecological validity\n704\t    nail: real web contamination involves diverse generators. In reframed paper, this\n705\t    honestly motivates future work on generator-invariant depth features and positions\n706\t    the current features as a baseline that works in controlled (single-generator)\n707\t    settings but not in the wild.\n708\t\n709\t    '\n710\t- name: multi_domain_seeds\n711\t  plan_id: plan_008\n712\t  description: 'Expand to 4 domains. In worst tier, significant domain variance with\n713\t    some domains showing near-chance performance.\n714\t\n715\t    '\n716\t  expected_results:\n717\t    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.72\n718\t    news_macro_f1: 0.68\n719\t    scientific_macro_f1: 0.55\n720\t    creative_macro_f1: 0.48\n721\t    overall_macro_f1: 0.61\n722\t    cross_domain_train_test_f1: 0.49\n723\t    domain_f1_std: 0.11\n724\t  result_rationale: 'Domain variance is substantial. Wikipedia and news seeds (factual,\n725\t    structured prose) show passable performance because LLMs generate predictable\n726\t    continuations with consistent distributional drift. Scientific abstracts show\n727\t    weaker signal because the specialized vocabulary and formulaic structure reduce\n728\t    the distinguishability of human vs machine text at each depth. Creative writing\n729\t    shows near-chance performance because it has the highest natural language variance\n730\t    — the within-class variation in features (e.g., TTR, hapax_ratio) for creative\n731\t    text at any depth is large enough to swamp the between-class (depth) variation.\n732\t    Cross-domain transfer is poor (0.49) because the feature baselines differ across\n733\t    domains. This adds another moderator (domain + prompt type + generator family)\n734\t    that limits the generalizability of depth estimation.\n735\t\n736\t    '\n737\t  tables:\n738\t  - name: 'Table 14: Depth Classification by Domain'\n739\t    columns:\n740\t    - domain\n741\t    - n_seeds\n742\t    - macro_f1\n743\t    - gen0_f1\n744\t    - gen1_f1\n745\t    - gen2plus_f1\n746\t    rows:\n747\t    - - wikipedia\n748\t      - 500\n749\t      - 0.72\n750\t      - 0.89\n751\t      - 0.63\n752\t      - 0.64\n753\t    - - news\n754\t      - 500\n755\t      - 0.68\n756\t      - 0.86\n757\t      - 0.59\n758\t      - 0.59\n759\t    - - scientific\n760\t      - 500\n761\t      - 0.55\n762\t      - 0.78\n763\t      - 0.44\n764\t      - 0.43\n765\t    - - creative\n766\t      - 500\n767\t      - 0.48\n768\t      - 0.71\n769\t      - 0.37\n770\t      - 0.36\n771\t    - - all_pooled\n772\t      - 2000\n773\t      - 0.61\n774\t      - 0.81\n775\t      - 0.51\n776\t      - 0.51\n777\t  figures_description:\n778\t  - 'Fig 12: Feature variance decomposition by domain. Box plots showing much wider\n779\t    feature distributions for creative/scientific than wiki/news.'\n780\t  analysis: 'Domain is a significant moderator. Factual domains have more signal;\n781\t    creative domains have less. In reframed paper: \"Depth estimation difficulty depends\n782\t    on the entropy of the source domain — low-entropy domains (wiki, news) produce\n783\t    more predictable generations with clearer depth drift; high-entropy domains (creative)\n784\t    mask depth signal with natural variation.\"\n785\t\n786\t    '\n787\t- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n788\t  plan_id: plan_009\n789\t  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4). In worst tier,\n790\t    performance is near chance for finer classes.\n791\t\n792\t    '\n793\t  expected_results:\n794\t    five_class_macro_f1: 0.28\n795\t    three_class_macro_f1: 0.78\n796\t    gen0_f1: 0.85\n797\t    gen1_f1: 0.42\n798\t    gen2_f1: 0.18\n799\t    gen3_f1: 0.12\n800\t    gen4_f1: 0.11\n801\t    ordinal_mae: 1.4\n802\t    adjacent_confusion_pct: 0.72\n803\t  result_rationale: 'Five-class F1 of 0.28 is barely above chance (0.20 for 5 classes).\n804\t    Gen-0 remains detectable (F1=0.85) and gen-1 is marginally separable (F1=0.42),\n805\t    but gen-2/3/4 collapse into an indistinguishable cluster (F1: 0.18/0.12/0.11).\n806\t    This is because distributional drift between adjacent deep generations is minimal\n807\t    — the marginal change from gen-2→gen-3 in any feature is smaller than the within-class\n808\t    variance. The ordinal MAE of 1.4 means predictions are off by ~1.4 depth levels\n809\t    on average. 72% of misclassifications are between adjacent classes, suggesting\n810\t    some ordinal structure remains but is too weak for practical 5-class discrimination.\n811\t    This validates the attractor convergence prediction: beyond ~3 classes, depth\n812\t    signal is negligible even for non-paraphrastic chains.\n813\t\n814\t    '\n815\t  tables:\n816\t  - name: 'Table 15: Five-Class Ordinal Classification'\n817\t    columns:\n818\t    - class\n819\t    - precision\n820\t    - recall\n821\t    - f1\n822\t    - n_samples\n823\t    rows:\n824\t    - - gen-0\n825\t      - 0.88\n826\t      - 0.82\n827\t      - 0.85\n828\t      - 995\n829\t    - - gen-1\n830\t      - 0.39\n831\t      - 0.45\n832\t      - 0.42\n833\t      - 993\n834\t    - - gen-2\n835\t      - 0.16\n836\t      - 0.2\n837\t      - 0.18\n838\t      - 912\n839\t    - - gen-3\n840\t      - 0.11\n841\t      - 0.13\n842\t      - 0.12\n843\t      - 856\n844\t    - - gen-4\n845\t      - 0.1\n846\t      - 0.12\n847\t      - 0.11\n848\t      - 816\n849\t  figures_description:\n850\t  - 'Fig 13: 5×5 confusion matrix. Strong diagonal for gen-0; gen-1 somewhat visible;\n851\t    gen-2/3/4 form a diffuse block with heavy off-diagonal mass.'\n852\t  analysis: 'Five-class is essentially unsolvable with current features. Confirms\n853\t    the 3-class ceiling. In reframed paper, this is a benchmark result: \"We establish\n854\t    that 3-class depth estimation (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) is the practical ceiling for\n855\t    distributional features, consistent with attractor convergence theory extended\n856\t    to non-paraphrastic generation.\"\n857\t\n858\t    '\n859\t- name: human_edit_robustness\n860\t  plan_id: plan_010\n861\t  description: 'Robustness to simulated human edits. In worst tier, even mild edits\n862\t    substantially degrade performance.\n863\t\n864\t    '\n865\t  expected_results:\n866\t    baseline_f1: 0.78\n867\t    random_replace_5pct_f1: 0.68\n868\t    random_replace_10pct_f1: 0.58\n869\t    random_replace_20pct_f1: 0.44\n870\t    llm_light_edit_f1: 0.53\n871\t    f1_drop_at_5pct: -0.1\n872\t  result_rationale: 'Even 5% random word replacement drops F1 by 0.10, and LLM-simulated\n873\t    light editing drops F1 by 0.25. The features are brittle to post-generation modification\n874\t    because both surface features (TTR, hapax) and reference features (entropy statistics)\n875\t    are sensitive to token-level changes. A few word substitutions can significantly\n876\t    alter hapax ratio, TTR, and the reference model''s NLL distribution. LLM-simulated\n877\t    editing is worse than random replacement because it systematically \"cleans up\"\n878\t    the distributional artifacts that depth estimation relies on — an LLM editing\n879\t    gen-3 text makes it look more like gen-1 by smoothing out the accumulated drift.\n880\t    This is a fundamental limitation: any real-world post-processing pipeline (human\n881\t    editing, automated cleanup, grammar correction) will degrade depth signal.\n882\t\n883\t    '\n884\t  tables:\n885\t  - name: 'Table 16: Robustness to Human Edits'\n886\t    columns:\n887\t    - edit_type\n888\t    - edit_rate\n889\t    - macro_f1\n890\t    - delta_f1\n891\t    rows:\n892\t    - - none\n893\t      - 0%\n894\t      - 0.78\n895\t      - '-'\n896\t    - - random_replace\n897\t      - 5%\n898\t      - 0.68\n899\t      - -0.1\n900\t    - - random_replace\n901\t      - 10%\n902\t      - 0.58\n903\t      - -0.2\n904\t    - - random_replace\n905\t      - 20%\n906\t      - 0.44\n907\t      - -0.34\n908\t    - - llm_light_edit\n909\t      - ~8%\n910\t      - 0.53\n911\t      - -0.25\n912\t  figures_description:\n913\t  - 'Fig 14: F1 degradation curve as function of edit rate. Roughly linear decline\n914\t    with slope ≈-1.7 F1 per percentage point of edits.'\n915\t  analysis: 'Depth features are fragile to post-generation modification. In reframed\n916\t    paper: \"Depth estimation is most applicable to unedited generation chains (e.g.,\n917\t    automated content farms) and degrades rapidly with human post-processing. This\n918\t    constrains the practical applicability to detection of fully automated pipelines.\"\n919\t\n920\t    '\n921\t- name: ld_score_feature\n922\t  plan_id: plan_011\n923\t  description: 'Add LD-Score as feature. In worst tier, LD-Score shows inconsistent\n924\t    behavior — neither clearly H2 nor clearly adding value.\n925\t\n926\t    '\n927\t  expected_results:\n928\t    ldscore_depth_rho: -0.25\n929\t    ldscore_added_f1_delta: 0.005\n930\t    ldscore_h2_classification: weak/ambiguous\n931\t    with_ldscore_f1: 0.785\n932\t    without_ldscore_f1: 0.78\n933\t  result_rationale: 'LD-Score shows weak depth correlation (ρ=-0.25), not clearly\n934\t    meeting H2 criteria (slope significant but R²<0.15). Character-level distribution\n935\t    is a noisy proxy for depth — it captures some vocabulary shift but is contaminated\n936\t    by topic and style variation. Adding LD-Score to the feature set provides negligible\n937\t    improvement (+0.005 F1), well within noise. The pre-registered prediction that\n938\t    LD-Score would be a \"level feature\" exhibiting H2 drift is partially supported\n939\t    (negative ρ) but weak. In worst tier, this fails to provide out-of-sample validation\n940\t    for the level/shape framework because the framework itself is collapsing (plan_003\n941\t    shows H2 is reference-model-dependent).\n942\t\n943\t    '\n944\t  tables:\n945\t  - name: 'Table 17: LD-Score as Depth Feature'\n946\t    columns:\n947\t    - metric\n948\t    - value\n949\t    rows:\n950\t    - - ldscore_depth_rho\n951\t      - -0.25\n952\t    - - ldscore_slope_R2\n953\t      - 0.12\n954\t    - - feature_set_without_ldscore_f1\n955\t      - 0.78\n956\t    - - feature_set_with_ldscore_f1\n957\t      - 0.785\n958\t    - - ldscore_feature_importance_rank\n959\t      - 7/8\n960\t  figures_description:\n961\t  - 'Fig 15: LD-Score by depth. Noisy downward trend with wide variance bands. Partial\n962\t    H2 behavior but unconvincing.'\n963\t  analysis: 'LD-Score pre-registered prediction is weakly supported but insufficient\n964\t    to validate the level/shape framework given that the framework is independently\n965\t    undermined by plan_003. In reframed paper: \"LD-Score shows weak depth sensitivity,\n966\t    consistent with character-level features capturing some vocabulary drift, but\n967\t    insufficient to serve as independent validation of the dual dynamics hypothesis.\"\n968\t\n969\t    '\n970\t- name: end_to_end_classifier\n971\t  plan_id: plan_012\n972\t  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification.\n973\t    In worst tier, end-to-end slightly outperforms handcrafted features, undermining\n974\t    the feature analysis.\n975\t\n976\t    '\n977\t  expected_results:\n978\t    e2e_macro_f1: 0.83\n979\t    handcrafted_macro_f1: 0.78\n980\t    e2e_cross_generator_f1: 0.55\n981\t    e2e_vs_handcrafted_delta: 0.05\n982\t    e2e_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.76\n983\t  result_rationale: 'End-to-end classification achieves F1=0.83, slightly above the\n984\t    handcrafted 0.78, suggesting that learned representations capture additional signal\n985\t    beyond the 7 distributional features. However, the improvement is modest and the\n986\t    end-to-end model also fails to generalize cross-generator (F1=0.55), indicating\n987\t    the additional signal is also generator-specific. This undercuts the feature analysis\n988\t    contribution: if a fine-tuned LM can slightly beat the handcrafted features, the\n989\t    dual dynamics decomposition is more of an interpretability lens than a competitive\n990\t    method. In worst tier, this result slightly undermines the feature-level analysis\n991\t    without providing a clearly superior alternative — neither approach generalizes.\n992\t\n993\t    '\n994\t  tables:\n995\t  - name: 'Table 18: End-to-End vs Handcrafted Features'\n996\t    columns:\n997\t    - method\n998\t    - in_dist_f1\n999\t    - cross_gen_logo_f1\n1000\t    - training_cost_gpu_h\n1001\t    rows:\n1002\t    - - handcrafted_xgboost\n1003\t      - 0.78\n1004\t      - 0.52\n1005\t      - 0.1\n1006\t    - - qwen_qolra_e2e\n1007\t      - 0.83\n1008\t      - 0.55\n1009\t      - 15\n1010\t    - - qwen_qolra_frozen\n1011\t      - 0.8\n1012\t      - 0.53\n1013\t      - 8\n1014\t  figures_description:\n1015\t  - 'Fig 16: Learning curves for end-to-end model showing rapid convergence on in-distribution\n1016\t    data. Cross-generator performance plateaus early.'\n1017\t  analysis: 'End-to-end slightly beats handcrafted but shares the generalization failure.\n1018\t    In reframed paper: \"Both handcrafted and learned features achieve similar in-distribution\n1019\t    performance and similar cross-generator degradation, suggesting the depth estimation\n1020\t    difficulty is fundamental to the feature space, not a limitation of our specific\n1021\t    feature engineering.\"\n1022\t\n1023\t    '\n1024\t- name: text_length_control\n1025\t  plan_id: plan_013\n1026\t  description: 'Text length confound analysis. In worst tier, length-controlled F1\n1027\t    drops >0.08, revealing that surface features substantially tracked length.\n1028\t\n1029\t    '\n1030\t  expected_results:\n1031\t    length_depth_correlation: 0.42\n1032\t    original_full_f1: 0.78\n1033\t    length_controlled_full_f1: 0.68\n1034\t    length_controlled_f1_delta: -0.1\n1035\t    length_controlled_surface_f1: 0.63\n1036\t    length_controlled_reference_f1: 0.66\n1037\t    length_normalized_ttr_rho: -0.12\n1038\t    length_normalized_hapax_rho: -0.09\n1039\t    original_ttr_rho: -0.31\n1040\t    original_hapax_rho: -0.28\n1041\t    length_matched_f1: 0.7\n1042\t  result_rationale: 'Text length correlates with depth (r=0.42) — deeper generations\n1043\t    tend to be shorter as models produce more repetitive/compressed outputs. This\n1044\t    correlation confounds both surface features and indirectly reference features.\n1045\t    TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically length-dependent (shorter texts have higher\n1046\t    TTR, lower hapax ratio), so their depth correlation (ρ≈-0.3) was partially a length\n1047\t    proxy. After length normalization (fixed-window computation), TTR depth correlation\n1048\t    drops from -0.31 to -0.12 and hapax from -0.28 to -0.09 — most of the \"H2 drift\"\n1049\t    in surface features was length artifact. Length-controlled full F1 drops by 0.10\n1050\t    (from 0.78 to 0.68), breaching the >0.08 threshold. Notably, reference features\n1051\t    also drop (from 0.72 to 0.66) because entropy statistics are affected by text\n1052\t    length through the number of tokens evaluated. After length matching (subsampling\n1053\t    to equalize length distributions), F1 is 0.70 — real depth signal exists but is\n1054\t    substantially weaker than originally measured. The full-vs-surface gap shrinks\n1055\t    (0.68 vs 0.63 = 0.05 gap → 0.02 gap after removing length confound from surface\n1056\t    features).\n1057\t\n1058\t    '\n1059\t  tables:\n1060\t  - name: 'Table 19: Length Confound Analysis'\n1061\t    columns:\n1062\t    - condition\n1063\t    - full_f1\n1064\t    - surface_f1\n1065\t    - reference_f1\n1066\t    - full_vs_surface_gap\n1067\t    rows:\n1068\t    - - original\n1069\t      - 0.78\n1070\t      - 0.76\n1071\t      - 0.72\n1072\t      - 0.02\n1073\t    - - length_normalized_features\n1074\t      - 0.68\n1075\t      - 0.63\n1076\t      - 0.66\n1077\t      - 0.02\n1078\t    - - length_matched_subsampling\n1079\t      - 0.7\n1080\t      - 0.65\n1081\t      - 0.67\n1082\t      - 0.03\n1083\t  - name: 'Table 20: Feature Depth Correlation Before/After Length Control'\n1084\t    columns:\n1085\t    - feature\n1086\t    - original_rho\n1087\t    - length_normalized_rho\n1088\t    - pct_explained_by_length\n1089\t    rows:\n1090\t    - - ttr\n1091\t      - -0.31\n1092\t      - -0.12\n1093\t      - 61%\n1094\t    - - hapax_ratio\n1095\t      - -0.28\n1096\t      - -0.09\n1097\t      - 68%\n1098\t    - - entropy_mean\n1099\t      - -0.68\n1100\t      - -0.55\n1101\t      - 19%\n1102\t    - - entropy_std\n1103\t      - 0.03\n1104\t      - 0.02\n1105\t      - 33%\n1106\t    - - tail_decay\n1107\t      - 0.04\n1108\t      - 0.03\n1109\t      - 25%\n1110\t  figures_description:\n1111\t  - 'Fig 17: Text length distributions by depth. Clear shift toward shorter texts\n1112\t    at deeper generations.'\n1113\t  - 'Fig 18: TTR vs depth before/after length normalization. Original shows clear\n1114\t    decline; normalized shows near-flat with slight residual trend.'\n1115\t  analysis: 'Length confound is substantial. ~60-68% of surface feature depth correlation\n1116\t    was length artifact. Reference features are less affected (19% for entropy_mean)\n1117\t    but still impacted. After controlling for length, the full-vs-surface gap nearly\n1118\t    vanishes (0.02), meaning reference features'' advantage was partially from being\n1119\t    less length-confounded, not from capturing genuinely different depth signal. In\n1120\t    reframed paper: \"We identify text length as a major confound in depth estimation:\n1121\t    standard surface diversity metrics (TTR, hapax ratio) primarily track length variation\n1122\t    rather than genuine distributional drift. Length-controlled analysis reveals a\n1123\t    weaker but real residual depth signal, predominantly carried by reference-model\n1124\t    perplexity statistics.\"\n1125\t\n1126\t    '\n1127\t- name: large_scale_generator\n1128\t  plan_id: plan_014\n1129\t  description: 'Add 7B+ generator (Qwen3-8B-Base). In worst tier, depth signal is\n1130\t    minimal at this scale — larger models produce more fluent text that masks distributional\n1131\t    drift.\n1132\t\n1133\t    '\n1134\t  expected_results:\n1135\t    generator_7bplus_macro_f1: 0.43\n1136\t    generator_7bplus_gen0_f1: 0.62\n1137\t    generator_7bplus_gen1_f1: 0.35\n1138\t    generator_7bplus_gen2plus_f1: 0.32\n1139\t    generator_7bplus_entropy_mean_rho: -0.19\n1140\t    small_generator_avg_f1: 0.72\n1141\t    f1_vs_generator_size_correlation: -0.85\n1142\t    generator_7bplus_ppl_depth0: 8.2\n1143\t    generator_7bplus_ppl_depth4: 4.1\n1144\t    ppl_dynamic_range_7bplus: 4.1\n1145\t    ppl_dynamic_range_2_8b: 11.8\n1146\t  result_rationale: 'The 7B+ generator produces text that is much harder to depth-classify\n1147\t    (F1=0.43, near chance for gen-1 and gen-2+). Several mechanisms explain this:\n1148\t    (1) larger models have lower perplexity under any reference model, so the absolute\n1149\t    perplexity at gen-0 is already closer to machine-generated (8.2 vs 13.5 for human\n1150\t    text under Pythia-2.8B reference), reducing the dynamic range for drift detection\n1151\t    (4.1 vs 11.8 points of perplexity range across depths); (2) larger models maintain\n1152\t    more vocabulary diversity across recursive generations — TTR and hapax ratio drift\n1153\t    less because the model''s larger capacity preserves lexical variety; (3) the distributional\n1154\t    shape of 7B+ outputs is more \"human-like\" at every depth, compressing the feature\n1155\t    space. The F1-vs-generator-size correlation of -0.85 shows a clear scaling law:\n1156\t    depth discriminability degrades roughly log-linearly with generator parameters.\n1157\t    This connects directly to model collapse literature — larger models collapse slower,\n1158\t    producing less distributional drift per recursive step. Gen-0 detection (human\n1159\t    vs 7B-generated) is also harder (F1=0.62 vs ~0.90 for smaller generators) because\n1160\t    7B outputs are more human-like on all features.\n1161\t\n1162\t    '\n1163\t  tables:\n1164\t  - name: 'Table 21: Depth Classification by Generator Scale'\n1165\t    columns:\n1166\t    - generator\n1167\t    - params\n1168\t    - macro_f1\n1169\t    - gen0_f1\n1170\t    - gen1_f1\n1171\t    - gen2plus_f1\n1172\t    - entropy_mean_rho\n1173\t    rows:\n1174\t    - - qwen3-0.6b\n1175\t      - 0.6B\n1176\t      - 0.74\n1177\t      - 0.91\n1178\t      - 0.66\n1179\t      - 0.65\n1180\t      - -0.58\n1181\t    - - pythia-1b\n1182\t      - 1B\n1183\t      - 0.71\n1184\t      - 0.89\n1185\t      - 0.62\n1186\t      - 0.62\n1187\t      - -0.55\n1188\t    - - olmo-2-1b\n1189\t      - 1.5B\n1190\t      - 0.69\n1191\t      - 0.87\n1192\t      - 0.6\n1193\t      - 0.6\n1194\t      - -0.51\n1195\t    - - qwen3-1.7b\n1196\t      - 1.7B\n1197\t      - 0.68\n1198\t      - 0.86\n1199\t      - 0.58\n1200\t      - 0.6\n1201\t      - -0.48\n1202\t    - - pythia-2.8b\n1203\t      - 2.8B\n1204\t      - 0.78\n1205\t      - 0.93\n1206\t      - 0.72\n1207\t      - 0.69\n1208\t      - -0.68\n1209\t    - - qwen3-8b\n1210\t      - 8B\n1211\t      - 0.43\n1212\t      - 0.62\n1213\t      - 0.35\n1214\t      - 0.32\n1215\t      - -0.19\n1216\t  - name: 'Table 22: Perplexity Dynamic Range by Generator Scale'\n1217\t    columns:\n1218\t    - generator\n1219\t    - ppl_depth0\n1220\t    - ppl_depth4\n1221\t    - dynamic_range\n1222\t    - ppl_ratio\n1223\t    rows:\n1224\t    - - pythia-2.8b\n1225\t      - 13.5\n1226\t      - 1.7\n1227\t      - 11.8\n1228\t      - 7.9\n1229\t    - - pythia-1b\n1230\t      - 12.8\n1231\t      - 2.1\n1232\t      - 10.7\n1233\t      - 6.1\n1234\t    - - olmo-2-1b\n1235\t      - 11.4\n1236\t      - 2.5\n1237\t      - 8.9\n1238\t      - 4.6\n1239\t    - - qwen3-1.7b\n1240\t      - 10.8\n1241\t      - 2.8\n1242\t      - 8.0\n1243\t      - 3.9\n1244\t    - - qwen3-8b\n1245\t      - 8.2\n1246\t      - 4.1\n1247\t      - 4.1\n1248\t      - 2.0\n1249\t  figures_description:\n1250\t  - 'Fig 19: Macro-F1 vs log(generator_params). Clear negative log-linear trend with\n1251\t    R²=0.72. Pythia-2.8B is an outlier above the trend (same-family reference advantage).'\n1252\t  - 'Fig 20: Perplexity-by-depth curves for all generators. Smaller generators show\n1253\t    steeper descent; 7B+ shows shallow, nearly flat curve.'\n1254\t  - 'Fig 21: Feature space visualization (PCA) for 7B+ generator outputs. Depth classes\n1255\t    heavily overlap, unlike the clean separation seen with smaller generators.'\n1256\t  analysis: 'Scale-dependent depth signal degradation is the strongest negative finding\n1257\t    and the most publishable result in the worst tier. The log-linear F1-vs-scale\n1258\t    relationship is novel empirical evidence connecting depth estimation to model\n1259\t    collapse theory: \"larger models collapse slower (Gerstgrasser et al.), and correspondingly,\n1260\t    depth signal degrades with scale.\" Note Pythia-2.8B is anomalously high due to\n1261\t    same-family reference advantage — once corrected (using cross-family reference,\n1262\t    plan_003), it would likely fall closer to the trend line. The perplexity dynamic\n1263\t    range analysis (Table 22) provides a mechanistic explanation: reference-model\n1264\t    perplexity has less room to drift when the generator is already fluent. In reframed\n1265\t    paper, this becomes the central analytical contribution: \"Generation depth estimation\n1266\t    exhibits a scale-dependent regime: distributional features that reliably detect\n1267\t    depth for ≤3B generators fail at 7B+ scale, providing empirical evidence for scale-dependent\n1268\t    convergence rates in recursive generation.\"'\n1269\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a5f594e8-570b-47df-b34a-5c33d75d6e0f","uuid":"9768b505-841a-4847-bf61-1c5f3e015c35","timestamp":"2026-05-14T23:06:53.777Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_3/projects/gen-depth-fingerprint/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T221538/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Abandon dual dynamics as headline contribution. Reframe paper\n  around two angles that survive: (1) Task formulation paper — ''Generation Depth\n  Estimation: A Missing Layer in Synthetic Text Analysis.'' The task itself (ordinal\n  depth, not binary) is novel regardless of feature performance. Formalize the task,\n  provide baselines, release benchmark dataset, discuss what makes it hard. (2) Negative/analytical\n  result on depth signal scaling — depth signal is strong for small generators but\n  degrades with scale, revealing that distributional drift from recursive generation\n  is partially masked by model fluency. Connects to model collapse literature. Perplexity-by-depth\n  monotonic decrease (13.5→1.7) remains robust as reference-model phenomenon — reposition\n  around ''reference-model perplexity as depth proxy'' rather than the full dual dynamics\n  story. Paper becomes: new task + benchmark + scaling analysis + practical baseline.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: feature_ablation_and_trend\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Feature ablation (surface-only vs reference-only vs full) and H1/H2\n    trend analysis on MVP data. Already executed; registry results from plan-001-fix\n    are taken as given. In worst tier, these in-distribution results are not wrong\n    but are misleadingly optimistic — the 0.06 full-vs-surface gap and the clean H1/H2\n    split both depend on same-family (Pythia) reference and single-generator setting.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    full_macro_f1: 0.8204\n    surface_only_f1: 0.7628\n    reference_only_f1: 0.7187\n    full_vs_surface_gap: 0.058\n    full_vs_reference_gap: 0.102\n    h2_features_count: 3\n    h1_features_count: 4\n    entropy_mean_depth_rho: -0.68\n    median_ppl_by_depth: 13.50, 4.96, 3.00, 2.07, 1.72\n  result_rationale: 'These results are from actual execution and look encouraging.\n    But they are entirely in-distribution: single generator (Pythia-2.8B), same-family\n    reference (Pythia-410M), no length control. The 0.82 full F1 benefits from generator-specific\n    artifacts that the reference model can detect precisely because it shares the\n    Pythia architecture. The H2 entropy_mean drift (ρ=-0.68) is strong here because\n    Pythia-410M and Pythia-2.8B share tokenizer and distributional priors — the reference\n    model''s perplexity is calibrated to the generator''s output distribution. The\n    perplexity monotonic decrease (13.5→1.7) is real but partially reflects the reference\n    model''s familiarity with same-family outputs. In worst tier, subsequent experiments\n    reveal these as inflated baselines.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Feature Ablation (MVP In-Distribution)'\n    columns:\n    - feature_set\n    - macro_f1\n    - 95_ci\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    rows:\n    - - full\n      - 0.8204\n      - '[0.807, 0.834]'\n      - 0.952\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.795\n    - - surface_only\n      - 0.7628\n      - '[0.747, 0.774]'\n      - 0.92\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.658\n    - - reference_only\n      - 0.7187\n      - '[0.706, 0.733]'\n      - 0.895\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.621\n  - name: 'Table 2: H1/H2 Feature Classification'\n    columns:\n    - feature\n    - source\n    - depth_slope\n    - R2\n    - classification\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - reference\n      - -0.042\n      - 0.91\n      - H2_drift\n    - - ttr\n      - surface\n      - -0.031\n      - 0.87\n      - H2_drift\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - surface\n      - -0.028\n      - 0.83\n      - H2_drift\n    - - entropy_std\n      - reference\n      - 0.003\n      - 0.04\n      - H1_stationary\n    - - entropy_max\n      - reference\n      - -0.005\n      - 0.07\n      - H1_stationary\n    - - entropy_min\n      - reference\n      - 0.002\n      - 0.02\n      - H1_stationary\n    - - tail_decay\n      - reference\n      - 0.004\n      - 0.05\n      - H1_stationary\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 1: Feature trends across depth 0-4 with mean±std bands. Clear monotonic decrease\n    for entropy_mean/TTR/hapax_ratio; flat for shape features. Looks clean — but this\n    is single-generator same-family setting.'\n  - 'Fig 2: Perplexity by depth (Pythia-410M reference). Monotonic 13.5→1.7. Strong\n    signal — survives into worst tier as reference-model-specific phenomenon.'\n  analysis: 'In-distribution results are solid. The dual dynamics split appears clean:\n    3 H2 features drift, 4 H1 features are stationary. Perplexity signal is very strong.\n    However, the worst-tier interpretation is that this clean separation is partly\n    an artifact of the Pythia-Pythia configuration. Subsequent experiments (plan_003,\n    plan_013) will show the H2 drift is partially confounded by length artifacts and\n    reference-model specificity.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_generator_evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Leave-one-generator-out evaluation across 5 generators. Tests cross-generator\n    generalization. In worst tier, LOGO F1 is poor, especially when holding out non-Pythia\n    generators.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    overall_logo_macro_f1: 0.52\n    logo_holdout_pythia28b: 0.61\n    logo_holdout_pythia1b: 0.64\n    logo_holdout_olmo2: 0.48\n    logo_holdout_qwen17b: 0.44\n    logo_holdout_qwen06b: 0.43\n    within_family_avg_f1: 0.625\n    cross_family_avg_f1: 0.45\n    leave_one_family_out_pythia_held_in: 0.58\n    leave_one_family_out_pythia_held_out: 0.41\n  result_rationale: 'The classifier overfits to generator-specific artifacts rather\n    than learning universal depth signatures. Within the Pythia family, generalization\n    is passable (0.61–0.64) because Pythia-2.8B and Pythia-1B share tokenizer, training\n    data (The Pile), and architectural patterns — the Pythia-410M reference model''s\n    NLL features are structurally similar for both. But cross-family holdouts (OLMo,\n    Qwen) drop sharply to 0.43–0.48 because: (a) different tokenizers mean surface\n    features (TTR, hapax) have different baseline distributions, (b) the Pythia-410M\n    reference model assigns systematically different perplexity distributions to non-Pythia\n    outputs, creating a domain shift in entropy features, (c) each generator family\n    has its own repetition/diversity profile at each depth, so depth-correlated patterns\n    learned from one family don''t transfer. The leave-one-family-out result (0.41\n    when Pythia held out) confirms the classifier critically depends on Pythia-specific\n    patterns. Overall LOGO F1 of 0.52 is below the 0.58 threshold, killing the cross-generator\n    generalization claim.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Leave-One-Generator-Out Macro-F1'\n    columns:\n    - held_out_generator\n    - family\n    - macro_f1\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    rows:\n    - - pythia-2.8b\n      - pythia\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.51\n      - 0.5\n    - - pythia-1b\n      - pythia\n      - 0.64\n      - 0.85\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.53\n    - - olmo-2-1b\n      - olmo\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.34\n    - - qwen3-1.7b\n      - qwen\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.29\n    - - qwen3-0.6b\n      - qwen\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.33\n      - 0.29\n    - - mean (all)\n      - '-'\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.39\n  - name: 'Table 4: Leave-One-Family-Out'\n    columns:\n    - held_out_family\n    - train_generators\n    - macro_f1\n    rows:\n    - - pythia\n      - olmo+qwen\n      - 0.41\n    - - olmo\n      - pythia+qwen\n      - 0.54\n    - - qwen\n      - pythia+olmo\n      - 0.56\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 3: Confusion matrices for each LOGO fold. Pythia holdouts show reasonable\n    diagonal; OLMo/Qwen holdouts show heavy off-diagonal mass, especially gen1↔gen2+\n    confusion.'\n  - 'Fig 4: Feature distribution shift across generators. Box plots of entropy_mean\n    by depth for each generator — Pythia generators show clean separation, OLMo/Qwen\n    show overlapping distributions.'\n  analysis: 'Cross-generator generalization fails the 0.58 threshold. The within-family\n    vs cross-family gap (0.625 vs 0.45) reveals the classifier relies on generator-specific\n    artifacts. Gen-0 detection remains passable (~0.75 average) because human-written\n    text is universally distinct, but gen-1 vs gen-2+ discrimination (the core depth\n    signal) collapses cross-family. This undermines Claim 1''s generalization requirement.\n    In the reframed paper, this becomes evidence for: \"depth estimation requires generator-aware\n    features or larger, more diverse training sets — the task is harder than binary\n    detection because depth artifacts are generator-specific.\"\n\n    '\n- name: cross_family_reference_model\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Test non-Pythia reference models (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) for feature\n    extraction. Critical falsification test for the dual dynamics framework.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    olmo_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.22\n    qwen_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.15\n    pythia_ref_entropy_mean_rho: -0.68\n    olmo_ref_full_f1: 0.63\n    qwen_ref_full_f1: 0.58\n    pythia_ref_full_f1: 0.82\n    olmo_ref_h2_features_count: 1\n    qwen_ref_h2_features_count: 1\n    cross_family_ref_entropy_mean_rho_avg: -0.185\n    shape_features_h1_stable_across_refs: true\n    ttr_rho_across_refs: -0.3\n    hapax_rho_across_refs: -0.27\n  result_rationale: 'Entropy_mean drift (ρ=-0.68 with Pythia reference) largely disappears\n    with non-Pythia references (ρ≈-0.22 OLMo, ρ≈-0.15 Qwen). This happens because\n    entropy_mean is driven by how well the reference model predicts the generated\n    text — when the reference model shares architecture and training data with the\n    generator (Pythia-Pythia), it has calibrated predictions that systematically shift\n    with depth. A cross-family reference model assigns noisier, less calibrated perplexities,\n    washing out the drift signal. The |ρ|<0.3 threshold is breached for both non-Pythia\n    references. Shape features (entropy_std, entropy_max, tail_decay) remain H1-stationary\n    across all references — this is genuinely robust because distributional shape\n    is less sensitive to absolute calibration. But the H2 side of the \"dual dynamics\"\n    now has only surface features (TTR, hapax_ratio) showing reliable drift (ρ≈-0.28\n    to -0.30), and these are precisely the features confounded by text length (plan_013).\n    The \"dual dynamics cross-cuts surface/reference boundary\" claim collapses: the\n    cross-cutting was an artifact of Pythia-Pythia alignment. Classification F1 with\n    non-Pythia reference drops to 0.58–0.63 because the strongest H2 reference feature\n    is now noise.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: Entropy_mean Depth Correlation by Reference Model'\n    columns:\n    - reference_model\n    - family\n    - entropy_mean_rho\n    - p_value\n    - h2_classification\n    rows:\n    - - pythia-410m\n      - pythia\n      - -0.68\n      - <0.001\n      - H2_confirmed\n    - - olmo-2-1b\n      - olmo\n      - -0.22\n      - 0.031\n      - H2_weak (|ρ|<0.3)\n    - - qwen3-0.6b\n      - qwen\n      - -0.15\n      - 0.118\n      - H2_not_confirmed\n  - name: 'Table 6: Full Feature Set Classification by Reference Model'\n    columns:\n    - reference_model\n    - macro_f1\n    - surface_only_f1\n    - ref_only_f1\n    - full_vs_surface_gap\n    rows:\n    - - pythia-410m\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.06\n    - - olmo-2-1b\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.02\n    - - qwen3-0.6b\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.57\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.01\n  - name: 'Table 7: H1/H2 Stability Across Reference Models'\n    columns:\n    - feature\n    - pythia_class\n    - olmo_class\n    - qwen_class\n    - stable_across_refs\n    rows:\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - H2\n      - weak/NS\n      - NS\n      - 'NO'\n    - - entropy_std\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - 'YES'\n    - - entropy_max\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - 'YES'\n    - - tail_decay\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - H1\n      - 'YES'\n    - - ttr\n      - H2\n      - H2\n      - H2\n      - 'YES'\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - H2\n      - H2\n      - weak H2\n      - YES (marginal)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 5: Entropy_mean by depth for 3 reference models. Pythia shows clear monotonic\n    decrease; OLMo shows slight noisy decline; Qwen nearly flat. Devastating for the\n    dual dynamics narrative.'\n  - 'Fig 6: Shape features (entropy_std, tail_decay) by depth for 3 reference models.\n    All show flat trends — H1 stationarity is genuinely reference-model-independent.'\n  analysis: 'The key falsification test triggers: entropy_mean''s H2 drift was substantially\n    a Pythia-specific artifact. The dual dynamics narrative (\"level features drift,\n    shape features don''t, and this cross-cuts surface/reference\") collapses because\n    the cross-cutting evidence depended on entropy_mean being a reference feature\n    that drifts. Without it, H2 features are just surface features (TTR, hapax), and\n    H1 features are just reference features — exactly the naive expectation the paper\n    claimed to overturn. The shape feature H1 stability is real but less interesting:\n    it says \"distributional shape under any reference model is stable across depth,\"\n    which is a weaker claim. In the reframed paper, this becomes: \"reference-model\n    perplexity as depth proxy is reference-model-dependent — a same-family reference\n    provides much stronger signal, pointing toward an alignment-based explanation\n    of depth discriminability.\"\n\n    '\n- name: downstream_utility\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Fine-tune OLMo-1B on depth-stratified data. Compare depth-aware filtering\n    vs binary baselines with 3+ seeds and bootstrap CIs. In worst tier, the advantage\n    is not statistically significant.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    depth_quality_spearman: 0.28\n    depth_quality_spearman_ci: '[0.11, 0.45]'\n    depth_filtering_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.8\n    depth_filtering_ppl_improvement_ci: '[-0.5, 4.1]'\n    binary_filtering_ppl_improvement_pct: 1.2\n    binary_filtering_ppl_improvement_ci: '[-1.1, 3.5]'\n    depth_vs_binary_p_value: 0.31\n    sample_matched_depth_ppl: 14.2\n    sample_matched_binary_ppl: 14.5\n    sample_matched_p_value: 0.42\n    seed_variance_cv: 0.08\n    n_seeds: 5\n  result_rationale: 'With proper experimental controls (multiple seeds, CIs, sample-count\n    matching, non-Pythia model), the downstream advantage of depth-aware filtering\n    shrinks to statistical insignificance. Several factors contribute: (1) OLMo-1B\n    is more robust to training data contamination than Pythia-410M (the original MVP\n    model), reducing the overall signal magnitude; (2) with 5 seeds, the variance\n    across runs is substantial (CV≈0.08), swamping the small depth-vs-binary difference;\n    (3) the sample-count-matched comparison shows the original advantage was partly\n    a data size confound — depth-aware filtering keeps more data (it retains gen-1)\n    than binary filtering (which discards all synthetic), so the improvement was partially\n    from having more training data, not from depth stratification per se; (4) bootstrap\n    CIs for the depth-vs-binary comparison overlap substantially (p=0.31), failing\n    significance. The Spearman ρ=0.28 between predicted depth and quality degradation\n    is real but weak, falling in the [0.15, 0.4] worst-tier range. In isolation, this\n    correlation is interesting but not actionable. Claim 3 (downstream utility) cannot\n    be supported as \"significantly outperforms binary baselines.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: Downstream Fine-Tuning Results (OLMo-1B, 5 seeds)'\n    columns:\n    - filtering_strategy\n    - mean_ppl\n    - std_ppl\n    - 95_ci\n    - improvement_vs_unfiltered_pct\n    rows:\n    - - unfiltered\n      - 14.8\n      - 0.42\n      - '[14.2, 15.4]'\n      - '-'\n    - - binary_fastdetectgpt\n      - 14.5\n      - 0.51\n      - '[13.8, 15.2]'\n      - 1.2 [-1.1, 3.5]\n    - - binary_ldscore\n      - 14.6\n      - 0.48\n      - '[14.0, 15.2]'\n      - 0.9 [-1.4, 3.2]\n    - - depth_aware_keep_gen01\n      - 14.2\n      - 0.39\n      - '[13.7, 14.7]'\n      - 1.8 [-0.5, 4.1]\n    - - depth_aware_sample_matched\n      - 14.2\n      - 0.44\n      - '[13.6, 14.8]'\n      - 1.5 [-0.8, 3.8]\n  - name: 'Table 9: Depth-Quality Correlation'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - value\n    - 95_ci\n    rows:\n    - - spearman_rho\n      - 0.28\n      - '[0.11, 0.45]'\n    - - ppl_gen0_mean\n      - 12.1\n      - '[11.8, 12.4]'\n    - - ppl_gen1_mean\n      - 13.4\n      - '[12.9, 13.9]'\n    - - ppl_gen2_mean\n      - 14.1\n      - '[13.5, 14.7]'\n    - - ppl_gen3_mean\n      - 14.5\n      - '[13.8, 15.2]'\n    - - ppl_gen4_mean\n      - 14.9\n      - '[14.1, 15.7]'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 7: Box plots of held-out perplexity across 5 seeds for each filtering strategy.\n    Wide overlapping boxes; depth-aware slightly lower median but CIs encompass binary.'\n  - 'Fig 8: Training data depth composition vs downstream perplexity. Weak positive\n    trend (ρ=0.28) with substantial scatter.'\n  analysis: 'Downstream utility claim fails significance after proper experimental\n    controls. The improvement exists directionally (1.8% vs 1.2%) but CIs overlap.\n    This was the most anticipated outcome from dream reviews: single-run results are\n    fragile. In reframed paper, report honestly as \"directional evidence for depth-quality\n    correlation, insufficient for statistical significance at this sample size\" and\n    pivot to the correlation itself (ρ=0.28) as motivation for future work. The perplexity-by-depth\n    monotonic relationship (Table 9) is real and publishable as a descriptive finding\n    — it just doesn''t translate into significant downstream filtering advantage.\n\n    '\n- name: prompt_type_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Compare continuation vs elaboration vs inspired_rewriting chains.\n    In worst tier, depth signal varies dramatically by prompt type, undermining generalizability.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    continuation_macro_f1: 0.78\n    elaboration_macro_f1: 0.55\n    inspired_rewriting_macro_f1: 0.47\n    continuation_entropy_mean_rho: -0.65\n    elaboration_entropy_mean_rho: -0.38\n    rewriting_entropy_mean_rho: -0.18\n    cross_prompt_train_test_f1: 0.42\n  result_rationale: 'Depth signal is strongly prompt-type dependent. Continuation\n    chains preserve the strongest signal (F1=0.78) because each generation builds\n    directly on the previous output, creating cumulative distributional drift. Elaboration\n    chains weaken the signal (F1=0.55) because the model is asked to expand on a topic\n    rather than continue text — this introduces more creative variation that partially\n    resets the distributional state at each depth. Inspired rewriting shows near-chance\n    depth discrimination (F1=0.47) because the semantic departure from the source\n    is maximal — each depth step is essentially an independent generation conditioned\n    on a theme, not a recursive chain. The entropy_mean drift follows the same pattern\n    (ρ: -0.65/-0.38/-0.18). When training on one prompt type and testing on another,\n    F1 drops to 0.42, showing the features are prompt-type-specific. This means the\n    \"depth signal\" is partly a \"continuation-chain-specific\" artifact — real web contamination\n    involves diverse generation patterns, not just continuation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Depth Classification by Prompt Type'\n    columns:\n    - prompt_type\n    - macro_f1\n    - entropy_mean_rho\n    - ttr_rho\n    - gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1\n    rows:\n    - - continuation\n      - 0.78\n      - -0.65\n      - -0.29\n      - 0.71\n    - - elaboration\n      - 0.55\n      - -0.38\n      - -0.16\n      - 0.44\n    - - inspired_rewriting\n      - 0.47\n      - -0.18\n      - -0.08\n      - 0.36\n  - name: 'Table 11: Cross-Prompt Transfer'\n    columns:\n    - train_prompt\n    - test_prompt\n    - macro_f1\n    rows:\n    - - continuation\n      - elaboration\n      - 0.46\n    - - continuation\n      - rewriting\n      - 0.39\n    - - elaboration\n      - continuation\n      - 0.51\n    - - elaboration\n      - rewriting\n      - 0.42\n    - - rewriting\n      - continuation\n      - 0.44\n    - - rewriting\n      - elaboration\n      - 0.4\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 9: Feature drift (entropy_mean, TTR) by depth for 3 prompt types. Continuation\n    shows monotonic decrease; elaboration shows noisy partial decrease; rewriting\n    shows flat/noisy trajectory.'\n  analysis: 'Prompt type is a major moderator of depth signal, challenging ecological\n    validity. In the reframed paper, this becomes a positive finding for the task\n    formulation angle: \"Generation depth estimation difficulty depends critically\n    on the semantic coupling between generation steps. Continuation chains (high coupling)\n    preserve depth signal; rewriting chains (low coupling) do not. This connects to\n    attractor cycle theory — semantic-preserving chains converge, while semantically\n    divergent chains don''t accumulate distributional drift.\"\n\n    '\n- name: paraphrastic_comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Compare paraphrasing vs continuation chains. In worst tier, paraphrasing\n    chains converge quickly (as attractor theory predicts), but continuation chains\n    also show limited depth discrimination beyond gen-2.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    paraphrastic_3class_f1: 0.52\n    nonparaphrastic_3class_f1: 0.78\n    paraphrastic_entropy_mean_plateau_depth: 2\n    nonparaphrastic_saturation_depth: 3\n    paraphrastic_gen2_vs_gen3_f1: 0.34\n    nonparaphrastic_gen2_vs_gen3_f1: 0.41\n  result_rationale: 'Paraphrasing chains converge by depth 2 as attractor theory predicts\n    — gen-2, gen-3, gen-4 are nearly indistinguishable (F1=0.34 for gen-2 vs gen-3).\n    Continuation chains preserve more signal but also saturate by depth 3 — the distributional\n    drift accumulates but with diminishing marginal signal at each step. The worst-tier\n    interpretation: even non-paraphrastic chains have bounded depth discriminability,\n    just with a slightly higher ceiling than paraphrastic ones. The difference (0.78\n    vs 0.52) confirms continuation chains are better for depth estimation but doesn''t\n    rescue the broader generalizability problem — the signal is still chain-type-specific\n    and bounded.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Paraphrastic vs Non-Paraphrastic Depth Discrimination'\n    columns:\n    - chain_type\n    - 3class_f1\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    - entropy_mean_rho\n    rows:\n    - - paraphrastic\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.45\n      - 0.33\n      - -0.31\n    - - continuation\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.69\n      - -0.65\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 10: Feature values by depth for paraphrastic vs continuation chains. Paraphrastic\n    shows rapid convergence (plateau by depth 2); continuation shows gradual drift\n    with diminishing returns after depth 3.'\n  analysis: 'Confirms attractor theory prediction for paraphrastic chains. Continuation\n    chains have more signal but also saturate. In reframed paper: \"Our empirical results\n    are consistent with attractor convergence — extending the theory to non-paraphrastic\n    chains shows the convergence is slower but still present, bounding practical depth\n    estimation to ~3 useful classes.\"\n\n    '\n- name: cross_model_chains\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Cross-model chains (different generator per depth step). In worst\n    tier, cross-model chains destroy depth signal because generator identity dominates\n    depth identity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    cross_model_macro_f1: 0.39\n    single_model_macro_f1: 0.78\n    cross_model_gen0_f1: 0.65\n    cross_model_gen1_f1: 0.28\n    cross_model_gen2plus_f1: 0.24\n    cross_vs_single_delta: -0.39\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-model chains are dramatically harder (F1=0.39, essentially\n    near chance for gen-1 and gen-2+). The reason is fundamental: when generator identity\n    changes at each depth step, the distributional features capture generator identity\n    more than depth. A gen-1 text from Qwen looks more different from gen-1 text from\n    Pythia than gen-1 looks from gen-2 within the same generator. The features (NLL\n    statistics under a fixed reference model) are sensitive to the generator''s output\n    distribution, creating a confound where generator variation swamps depth variation.\n    Gen-0 remains detectable (F1=0.65) because human-written text is still distinct\n    from any machine-generated text, but ordinal depth discrimination collapses. This\n    is perhaps the strongest negative result: in the realistic scenario of diverse\n    web generators, depth estimation with these features is not viable.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: Single-Model vs Cross-Model Chains'\n    columns:\n    - chain_type\n    - macro_f1\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    rows:\n    - - single_model\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.69\n    - - cross_model_random\n      - 0.39\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.24\n    - - cross_model_increasing_size\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.27\n    - - cross_model_decreasing_size\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.27\n      - 0.24\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 11: t-SNE of feature space colored by depth, single-model (left) vs cross-model\n    (right). Single-model shows depth clusters; cross-model shows generator clusters.'\n  analysis: 'Cross-model chains destroy depth signal — generator identity is a much\n    stronger signal than depth in the feature space. This is the ecological validity\n    nail: real web contamination involves diverse generators. In reframed paper, this\n    honestly motivates future work on generator-invariant depth features and positions\n    the current features as a baseline that works in controlled (single-generator)\n    settings but not in the wild.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_domain_seeds\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Expand to 4 domains. In worst tier, significant domain variance with\n    some domains showing near-chance performance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    wikipedia_macro_f1: 0.72\n    news_macro_f1: 0.68\n    scientific_macro_f1: 0.55\n    creative_macro_f1: 0.48\n    overall_macro_f1: 0.61\n    cross_domain_train_test_f1: 0.49\n    domain_f1_std: 0.11\n  result_rationale: 'Domain variance is substantial. Wikipedia and news seeds (factual,\n    structured prose) show passable performance because LLMs generate predictable\n    continuations with consistent distributional drift. Scientific abstracts show\n    weaker signal because the specialized vocabulary and formulaic structure reduce\n    the distinguishability of human vs machine text at each depth. Creative writing\n    shows near-chance performance because it has the highest natural language variance\n    — the within-class variation in features (e.g., TTR, hapax_ratio) for creative\n    text at any depth is large enough to swamp the between-class (depth) variation.\n    Cross-domain transfer is poor (0.49) because the feature baselines differ across\n    domains. This adds another moderator (domain + prompt type + generator family)\n    that limits the generalizability of depth estimation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Depth Classification by Domain'\n    columns:\n    - domain\n    - n_seeds\n    - macro_f1\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    rows:\n    - - wikipedia\n      - 500\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.64\n    - - news\n      - 500\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.59\n      - 0.59\n    - - scientific\n      - 500\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.44\n      - 0.43\n    - - creative\n      - 500\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.37\n      - 0.36\n    - - all_pooled\n      - 2000\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.51\n      - 0.51\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 12: Feature variance decomposition by domain. Box plots showing much wider\n    feature distributions for creative/scientific than wiki/news.'\n  analysis: 'Domain is a significant moderator. Factual domains have more signal;\n    creative domains have less. In reframed paper: \"Depth estimation difficulty depends\n    on the entropy of the source domain — low-entropy domains (wiki, news) produce\n    more predictable generations with clearer depth drift; high-entropy domains (creative)\n    mask depth signal with natural variation.\"\n\n    '\n- name: finer_grained_ordinal\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: '5-class ordinal classification (gen-0 through gen-4). In worst tier,\n    performance is near chance for finer classes.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    five_class_macro_f1: 0.28\n    three_class_macro_f1: 0.78\n    gen0_f1: 0.85\n    gen1_f1: 0.42\n    gen2_f1: 0.18\n    gen3_f1: 0.12\n    gen4_f1: 0.11\n    ordinal_mae: 1.4\n    adjacent_confusion_pct: 0.72\n  result_rationale: 'Five-class F1 of 0.28 is barely above chance (0.20 for 5 classes).\n    Gen-0 remains detectable (F1=0.85) and gen-1 is marginally separable (F1=0.42),\n    but gen-2/3/4 collapse into an indistinguishable cluster (F1: 0.18/0.12/0.11).\n    This is because distributional drift between adjacent deep generations is minimal\n    — the marginal change from gen-2→gen-3 in any feature is smaller than the within-class\n    variance. The ordinal MAE of 1.4 means predictions are off by ~1.4 depth levels\n    on average. 72% of misclassifications are between adjacent classes, suggesting\n    some ordinal structure remains but is too weak for practical 5-class discrimination.\n    This validates the attractor convergence prediction: beyond ~3 classes, depth\n    signal is negligible even for non-paraphrastic chains.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Five-Class Ordinal Classification'\n    columns:\n    - class\n    - precision\n    - recall\n    - f1\n    - n_samples\n    rows:\n    - - gen-0\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.85\n      - 995\n    - - gen-1\n      - 0.39\n      - 0.45\n      - 0.42\n      - 993\n    - - gen-2\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.18\n      - 912\n    - - gen-3\n      - 0.11\n      - 0.13\n      - 0.12\n      - 856\n    - - gen-4\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.12\n      - 0.11\n      - 816\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 13: 5×5 confusion matrix. Strong diagonal for gen-0; gen-1 somewhat visible;\n    gen-2/3/4 form a diffuse block with heavy off-diagonal mass.'\n  analysis: 'Five-class is essentially unsolvable with current features. Confirms\n    the 3-class ceiling. In reframed paper, this is a benchmark result: \"We establish\n    that 3-class depth estimation (gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+) is the practical ceiling for\n    distributional features, consistent with attractor convergence theory extended\n    to non-paraphrastic generation.\"\n\n    '\n- name: human_edit_robustness\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Robustness to simulated human edits. In worst tier, even mild edits\n    substantially degrade performance.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    baseline_f1: 0.78\n    random_replace_5pct_f1: 0.68\n    random_replace_10pct_f1: 0.58\n    random_replace_20pct_f1: 0.44\n    llm_light_edit_f1: 0.53\n    f1_drop_at_5pct: -0.1\n  result_rationale: 'Even 5% random word replacement drops F1 by 0.10, and LLM-simulated\n    light editing drops F1 by 0.25. The features are brittle to post-generation modification\n    because both surface features (TTR, hapax) and reference features (entropy statistics)\n    are sensitive to token-level changes. A few word substitutions can significantly\n    alter hapax ratio, TTR, and the reference model''s NLL distribution. LLM-simulated\n    editing is worse than random replacement because it systematically \"cleans up\"\n    the distributional artifacts that depth estimation relies on — an LLM editing\n    gen-3 text makes it look more like gen-1 by smoothing out the accumulated drift.\n    This is a fundamental limitation: any real-world post-processing pipeline (human\n    editing, automated cleanup, grammar correction) will degrade depth signal.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 16: Robustness to Human Edits'\n    columns:\n    - edit_type\n    - edit_rate\n    - macro_f1\n    - delta_f1\n    rows:\n    - - none\n      - 0%\n      - 0.78\n      - '-'\n    - - random_replace\n      - 5%\n      - 0.68\n      - -0.1\n    - - random_replace\n      - 10%\n      - 0.58\n      - -0.2\n    - - random_replace\n      - 20%\n      - 0.44\n      - -0.34\n    - - llm_light_edit\n      - ~8%\n      - 0.53\n      - -0.25\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 14: F1 degradation curve as function of edit rate. Roughly linear decline\n    with slope ≈-1.7 F1 per percentage point of edits.'\n  analysis: 'Depth features are fragile to post-generation modification. In reframed\n    paper: \"Depth estimation is most applicable to unedited generation chains (e.g.,\n    automated content farms) and degrades rapidly with human post-processing. This\n    constrains the practical applicability to detection of fully automated pipelines.\"\n\n    '\n- name: ld_score_feature\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Add LD-Score as feature. In worst tier, LD-Score shows inconsistent\n    behavior — neither clearly H2 nor clearly adding value.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ldscore_depth_rho: -0.25\n    ldscore_added_f1_delta: 0.005\n    ldscore_h2_classification: weak/ambiguous\n    with_ldscore_f1: 0.785\n    without_ldscore_f1: 0.78\n  result_rationale: 'LD-Score shows weak depth correlation (ρ=-0.25), not clearly\n    meeting H2 criteria (slope significant but R²<0.15). Character-level distribution\n    is a noisy proxy for depth — it captures some vocabulary shift but is contaminated\n    by topic and style variation. Adding LD-Score to the feature set provides negligible\n    improvement (+0.005 F1), well within noise. The pre-registered prediction that\n    LD-Score would be a \"level feature\" exhibiting H2 drift is partially supported\n    (negative ρ) but weak. In worst tier, this fails to provide out-of-sample validation\n    for the level/shape framework because the framework itself is collapsing (plan_003\n    shows H2 is reference-model-dependent).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 17: LD-Score as Depth Feature'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - value\n    rows:\n    - - ldscore_depth_rho\n      - -0.25\n    - - ldscore_slope_R2\n      - 0.12\n    - - feature_set_without_ldscore_f1\n      - 0.78\n    - - feature_set_with_ldscore_f1\n      - 0.785\n    - - ldscore_feature_importance_rank\n      - 7/8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 15: LD-Score by depth. Noisy downward trend with wide variance bands. Partial\n    H2 behavior but unconvincing.'\n  analysis: 'LD-Score pre-registered prediction is weakly supported but insufficient\n    to validate the level/shape framework given that the framework is independently\n    undermined by plan_003. In reframed paper: \"LD-Score shows weak depth sensitivity,\n    consistent with character-level features capturing some vocabulary drift, but\n    insufficient to serve as independent validation of the dual dynamics hypothesis.\"\n\n    '\n- name: end_to_end_classifier\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'QLoRA fine-tuned Qwen3.5-0.8B for end-to-end depth classification.\n    In worst tier, end-to-end slightly outperforms handcrafted features, undermining\n    the feature analysis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    e2e_macro_f1: 0.83\n    handcrafted_macro_f1: 0.78\n    e2e_cross_generator_f1: 0.55\n    e2e_vs_handcrafted_delta: 0.05\n    e2e_gen1_vs_gen2plus_f1: 0.76\n  result_rationale: 'End-to-end classification achieves F1=0.83, slightly above the\n    handcrafted 0.78, suggesting that learned representations capture additional signal\n    beyond the 7 distributional features. However, the improvement is modest and the\n    end-to-end model also fails to generalize cross-generator (F1=0.55), indicating\n    the additional signal is also generator-specific. This undercuts the feature analysis\n    contribution: if a fine-tuned LM can slightly beat the handcrafted features, the\n    dual dynamics decomposition is more of an interpretability lens than a competitive\n    method. In worst tier, this result slightly undermines the feature-level analysis\n    without providing a clearly superior alternative — neither approach generalizes.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 18: End-to-End vs Handcrafted Features'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - in_dist_f1\n    - cross_gen_logo_f1\n    - training_cost_gpu_h\n    rows:\n    - - handcrafted_xgboost\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.1\n    - - qwen_qolra_e2e\n      - 0.83\n      - 0.55\n      - 15\n    - - qwen_qolra_frozen\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.53\n      - 8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 16: Learning curves for end-to-end model showing rapid convergence on in-distribution\n    data. Cross-generator performance plateaus early.'\n  analysis: 'End-to-end slightly beats handcrafted but shares the generalization failure.\n    In reframed paper: \"Both handcrafted and learned features achieve similar in-distribution\n    performance and similar cross-generator degradation, suggesting the depth estimation\n    difficulty is fundamental to the feature space, not a limitation of our specific\n    feature engineering.\"\n\n    '\n- name: text_length_control\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Text length confound analysis. In worst tier, length-controlled F1\n    drops >0.08, revealing that surface features substantially tracked length.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    length_depth_correlation: 0.42\n    original_full_f1: 0.78\n    length_controlled_full_f1: 0.68\n    length_controlled_f1_delta: -0.1\n    length_controlled_surface_f1: 0.63\n    length_controlled_reference_f1: 0.66\n    length_normalized_ttr_rho: -0.12\n    length_normalized_hapax_rho: -0.09\n    original_ttr_rho: -0.31\n    original_hapax_rho: -0.28\n    length_matched_f1: 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'Text length correlates with depth (r=0.42) — deeper generations\n    tend to be shorter as models produce more repetitive/compressed outputs. This\n    correlation confounds both surface features and indirectly reference features.\n    TTR and hapax_ratio are mechanically length-dependent (shorter texts have higher\n    TTR, lower hapax ratio), so their depth correlation (ρ≈-0.3) was partially a length\n    proxy. After length normalization (fixed-window computation), TTR depth correlation\n    drops from -0.31 to -0.12 and hapax from -0.28 to -0.09 — most of the \"H2 drift\"\n    in surface features was length artifact. Length-controlled full F1 drops by 0.10\n    (from 0.78 to 0.68), breaching the >0.08 threshold. Notably, reference features\n    also drop (from 0.72 to 0.66) because entropy statistics are affected by text\n    length through the number of tokens evaluated. After length matching (subsampling\n    to equalize length distributions), F1 is 0.70 — real depth signal exists but is\n    substantially weaker than originally measured. The full-vs-surface gap shrinks\n    (0.68 vs 0.63 = 0.05 gap → 0.02 gap after removing length confound from surface\n    features).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 19: Length Confound Analysis'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - full_f1\n    - surface_f1\n    - reference_f1\n    - full_vs_surface_gap\n    rows:\n    - - original\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.02\n    - - length_normalized_features\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.02\n    - - length_matched_subsampling\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.03\n  - name: 'Table 20: Feature Depth Correlation Before/After Length Control'\n    columns:\n    - feature\n    - original_rho\n    - length_normalized_rho\n    - pct_explained_by_length\n    rows:\n    - - ttr\n      - -0.31\n      - -0.12\n      - 61%\n    - - hapax_ratio\n      - -0.28\n      - -0.09\n      - 68%\n    - - entropy_mean\n      - -0.68\n      - -0.55\n      - 19%\n    - - entropy_std\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.02\n      - 33%\n    - - tail_decay\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.03\n      - 25%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 17: Text length distributions by depth. Clear shift toward shorter texts\n    at deeper generations.'\n  - 'Fig 18: TTR vs depth before/after length normalization. Original shows clear\n    decline; normalized shows near-flat with slight residual trend.'\n  analysis: 'Length confound is substantial. ~60-68% of surface feature depth correlation\n    was length artifact. Reference features are less affected (19% for entropy_mean)\n    but still impacted. After controlling for length, the full-vs-surface gap nearly\n    vanishes (0.02), meaning reference features'' advantage was partially from being\n    less length-confounded, not from capturing genuinely different depth signal. In\n    reframed paper: \"We identify text length as a major confound in depth estimation:\n    standard surface diversity metrics (TTR, hapax ratio) primarily track length variation\n    rather than genuine distributional drift. Length-controlled analysis reveals a\n    weaker but real residual depth signal, predominantly carried by reference-model\n    perplexity statistics.\"\n\n    '\n- name: large_scale_generator\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: 'Add 7B+ generator (Qwen3-8B-Base). In worst tier, depth signal is\n    minimal at this scale — larger models produce more fluent text that masks distributional\n    drift.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    generator_7bplus_macro_f1: 0.43\n    generator_7bplus_gen0_f1: 0.62\n    generator_7bplus_gen1_f1: 0.35\n    generator_7bplus_gen2plus_f1: 0.32\n    generator_7bplus_entropy_mean_rho: -0.19\n    small_generator_avg_f1: 0.72\n    f1_vs_generator_size_correlation: -0.85\n    generator_7bplus_ppl_depth0: 8.2\n    generator_7bplus_ppl_depth4: 4.1\n    ppl_dynamic_range_7bplus: 4.1\n    ppl_dynamic_range_2_8b: 11.8\n  result_rationale: 'The 7B+ generator produces text that is much harder to depth-classify\n    (F1=0.43, near chance for gen-1 and gen-2+). Several mechanisms explain this:\n    (1) larger models have lower perplexity under any reference model, so the absolute\n    perplexity at gen-0 is already closer to machine-generated (8.2 vs 13.5 for human\n    text under Pythia-2.8B reference), reducing the dynamic range for drift detection\n    (4.1 vs 11.8 points of perplexity range across depths); (2) larger models maintain\n    more vocabulary diversity across recursive generations — TTR and hapax ratio drift\n    less because the model''s larger capacity preserves lexical variety; (3) the distributional\n    shape of 7B+ outputs is more \"human-like\" at every depth, compressing the feature\n    space. The F1-vs-generator-size correlation of -0.85 shows a clear scaling law:\n    depth discriminability degrades roughly log-linearly with generator parameters.\n    This connects directly to model collapse literature — larger models collapse slower,\n    producing less distributional drift per recursive step. Gen-0 detection (human\n    vs 7B-generated) is also harder (F1=0.62 vs ~0.90 for smaller generators) because\n    7B outputs are more human-like on all features.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 21: Depth Classification by Generator Scale'\n    columns:\n    - generator\n    - params\n    - macro_f1\n    - gen0_f1\n    - gen1_f1\n    - gen2plus_f1\n    - entropy_mean_rho\n    rows:\n    - - qwen3-0.6b\n      - 0.6B\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.65\n      - -0.58\n    - - pythia-1b\n      - 1B\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.62\n      - -0.55\n    - - olmo-2-1b\n      - 1.5B\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.6\n      - 0.6\n      - -0.51\n    - - qwen3-1.7b\n      - 1.7B\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.86\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.6\n      - -0.48\n    - - pythia-2.8b\n      - 2.8B\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.93\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.69\n      - -0.68\n    - - qwen3-8b\n      - 8B\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.32\n      - -0.19\n  - name: 'Table 22: Perplexity Dynamic Range by Generator Scale'\n    columns:\n    - generator\n    - ppl_depth0\n    - ppl_depth4\n    - dynamic_range\n    - ppl_ratio\n    rows:\n    - - pythia-2.8b\n      - 13.5\n      - 1.7\n      - 11.8\n      - 7.9\n    - - pythia-1b\n      - 12.8\n      - 2.1\n      - 10.7\n      - 6.1\n    - - olmo-2-1b\n      - 11.4\n      - 2.5\n      - 8.9\n      - 4.6\n    - - qwen3-1.7b\n      - 10.8\n      - 2.8\n      - 8.0\n      - 3.9\n    - - qwen3-8b\n      - 8.2\n      - 4.1\n      - 4.1\n      - 2.0\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fig 19: Macro-F1 vs log(generator_params). Clear negative log-linear trend with\n    R²=0.72. Pythia-2.8B is an outlier above the trend (same-family reference advantage).'\n  - 'Fig 20: Perplexity-by-depth curves for all generators. Smaller generators show\n    steeper descent; 7B+ shows shallow, nearly flat curve.'\n  - 'Fig 21: Feature space visualization (PCA) for 7B+ generator outputs. Depth classes\n    heavily overlap, unlike the clean separation seen with smaller generators.'\n  analysis: 'Scale-dependent depth signal degradation is the strongest negative finding\n    and the most publishable result in the worst tier. The log-linear F1-vs-scale\n    relationship is novel empirical evidence connecting depth estimation to model\n    collapse theory: \"larger models collapse slower (Gerstgrasser et al.), and correspondingly,\n    depth signal degrades with scale.\" Note Pythia-2.8B is anomalously high due to\n    same-family reference advantage — once corrected (using cross-family reference,\n    plan_003), it would likely fall closer to the trend line. The perplexity dynamic\n    range analysis (Table 22) provides a mechanistic explanation: reference-model\n    perplexity has less room to drift when the generator is already fluent. In reframed\n    paper, this becomes the central analytical contribution: \"Generation depth estimation\n    exhibits a scale-dependent regime: distributional features that reliably detect\n    depth for ≤3B generators fail at 7B+ scale, providing empirical evidence for scale-dependent\n    convergence rates in recursive generation.\"'\n","numLines":1269,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1269}}}
